## WHY DO WE NEED CACHING ?
==============================

Suppose humare paas ek e-commerce application hai.

                              User
                                |
                                | HTTP Request
                                ↓
                         ┌───────────────┐
                         │  API Gateway  │
                         └───────┬───────┘
                                 |
                ┌────────────────┴────────────────┐
                ↓                                 ↓
        ┌─────────────────┐               ┌─────────────────┐
        │ Product Service │               │  Order Service  │
        │ Load Balancer   │               │ Load Balancer   │
        └────────┬────────┘               └────────┬────────┘
                 |                                 |
          ┌──────┼──────┐                   ┌──────┼──────┐
          ↓      ↓      ↓                   ↓      ↓      ↓
       ┌────┐ ┌────┐ ┌────┐              ┌────┐ ┌────┐ ┌────┐
       │ P1 │ │ P2 │ │ P3 │              │ O1 │ │ O2 │ │ O3 │
       └─┬──┘ └─┬──┘ └─┬──┘              └─┬──┘ └─┬──┘ └─┬──┘
         |      |      |                    |      |      |
         └──────┼──────┘                    └──────┼──────┘
                ↓                                  ↓
        ┌────────────────┐                 ┌────────────────┐
        │  Product DB    │                 │    Order DB    │
        └────────────────┘                 └────────────────┘


Yahan:

API Gateway
→ Request ko appropriate service ke Load Balancer tak route karta hai.

Service-specific Load Balancer
→ Us service ke multiple instances ke beech request distribute karta hai.

Service Instances
→ Actual business logic execute karte hain.

Database
→ Persistent data store karta hai.


## PROBLEM: REPEATED DATABASE READS
-------------------------------------

Suppose Product Service ke paas API hai:

    GET /products/101

User product 101 request karta hai.

Flow:

    User
      ↓
    API Gateway
      ↓
    Product Load Balancer
      ↓
    Product Service Instance
      ↓
    Product DB
      ↓
    Product Data
      ↓
    User


Ab maan lo Product 101 bahut popular hai.

1000 users same product request karte hain:

    GET /products/101
    GET /products/101
    GET /products/101
    ...

To theoretically:

    1000 Requests
          ↓
    1000 Database Reads


Problem:

Same data baar-baar Database se read kiya ja raha hai,
even though Product 101 ki information frequently change nahi ho rahi.


## IMPORTANT:
--------------

Caching ka primary benefit read-heavy workloads me hota hai,
especially jab same data frequently read kiya ja raha ho.

Example:

    GET /products/101
          ↓
    Repeated Reads
          ↓
       Cache
          ↓
    Database Reads Reduce


Lekin write operations ke case me:

    POST /orders
    PUT /products/101
    DELETE /products/101

Business data ko ultimately Database me update karna hi padega.

    Write Request
         ↓
      Application
         ↓
      Database


Caching generally Database writes ko eliminate nahi karti.


## WHY IS THIS A PROBLEM ?
---------------------------

Frequently accessed data ke liye har baar Database ko hit karna unnecessarily expensive ho sakta hai.

Har Database request ko process karne ke dauraan Database ke various resources consume hote hain.

Database operation me mainly following costs involved ho sakti hain:


1. NETWORK COMMUNICATION
-------------------------

Agar Application aur Database different servers par deployed hain:

    Application Server
            ↓
       Network / TCP
            ↓
      Database Server

Query Database Server tak network ke through jaati hai.

Database query process karke required result
network ke through wapas Application ko bhejta hai.

Isliye Application aur Database ke beech
network latency ka overhead hota hai.

Agar Application aur Database same server par deployed hain,
to communication machine ke andar hi hota hai across different
ports of the server.Physical network par data bahar nahi jaata,
lekin communication cost completely zero nahi hoti.


2. CPU USAGE
------------

Database query process karne ke liye CPU computation karta hai.

Query ke according database ko:

- Data locate karna
  → Database determine karta hai ki required row kis page me stored hai

- Filter karna
  → Example: WHERE price > 1000
    Sirf matching products select karna.

- Index lookup karna
  → Database check karta hai ki suitable index available hai ya nahi.
    Agar index available ho, to Query Optimizer decide karta hai
    ki index use karna beneficial hai ya nahi.
    Agar index use hota hai, to index ke through required row(s)
    ki location (relevant data page/block) efficiently locate ki ja sakti hai.

- Joins perform karna
  → Example: users JOIN orders

- Sorting karna
  → Example: ORDER BY price DESC

- Aggregation karna
  → Example: SUM(amount), COUNT(*), AVG(price)
    Multiple rows par calculation perform karna.

- Other required processing
  → Query ke according additional operations perform karna.

karni pad sakti hai.

Yaani same frequently requested data ke liye query baar-baar execute hone par database CPU resources repeatedly consume hote hain.


3. DATA ACCESS: DISK + RAM
----------------------------

Database ka persistent data disk (SSD / HDD) par stored hota hai.

Lekin Database directly har query ke liye disk se data read nahi karna chahta,
kyunki disk access RAM ke comparison me significantly slower hota hai.

Database frequently accessed data ko RAM ke
Buffer Pool me store kar sakta hai, taaki har baar
disk se data read na karna pade.

Database Buffer Pool = RAM ka woh area jahan Database
data/index pages ko temporarily store karta hai.

Database RAM ko fast working memory ke roop me use karta hai.

RAM me commonly:

    - Frequently accessed data pages
    - Required index pages
    - Query ke intermediate results
    - Modified (dirty) pages

store ho sakte hain.


## WHAT ARE PAGES / BLOCKS ?
-----------------------------

Database data ko generally fixed-size units,
called pages / blocks, me manage karta hai.

Data pages me actual table data stored hota hai.

Index pages me database indexes ka data stored hota hai,
jiska use Database required rows ko efficiently locate
karne ke liye karta hai.

Database query process karte waqt required
data pages aur index pages ko disk se RAM (Buffer Pool)
me load karta hai.


### HOW DOES DATA ACCESS WORK?
-------------------------------

Suppose query ko kisi particular data ki zarurat hai.

Database pehle check karta hai ki query ke liye required data aur, if applicable, index pages Buffer Pool (RAM) me already loaded hain ya nahi.

Agar required page already RAM me available hai:

    Query
      ↓
    Buffer Pool 
      ↓
    CPU Processing
      ↓
    Result

Database ko disk se dobara read karne ki
zarurat nahi padti.

Agar required page RAM me available nhi hai:

    Query
      ↓
    Required Page Buffer Pool  me nahi hai
      ↓
    DISK se Read
      ↓
    RAM / Buffer Pool me Load
      ↓
    CPU Processing
      ↓
    Result

Yahan Database ko pehle  disk se
required page RAM me load karna padta hai.


## WHY IS THIS A COST?
----------------------

1: RAM:
---------

Agar simultaneously bahut saari READ queries execute ho rahi hain,
to database ki available RAM par memory pressure increase ho sakta hai.
High memory pressure me database ko kuch data pages evict karke
RAM free karni pad sakti hai.
Agar baad me evicted page ki zarurat padti hai,
to database ko us page ko dobara storage se RAM me load karna pad sakta hai,
jisse additional I/O latency aur processing overhead hota hai.

2: DISK (SSD / HDD)
---------------------

Agar required data / index page RAM ke Buffer Pool me available nahi hai,
to Database ko disk se page read karna padta hai.
Disk I/O RAM access ke comparison me significantly slower hota hai,
isliye ye additional I/O latency aur processing overhead create karta hai.



